(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608131100")
  (:adr/title "annex custody: ten objects at one copy, two datasets found, and a push blocked by an archive flag")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-13")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/body "## Context

A prior session rescued 48 git-annex objects into orgs/cloud-itonami/kouhou from a temp
directory and md5-verified 48/48, then found that `git annex checkpresentkey <key> kotobase`
answered exit 0 for 38 of them and exit 1 for 10, stably across two runs, while
`git annex whereis` claimed all 48 had 3 copies. Two other datasets (product-corpus,
cloud-itonami-contact-pii) could not be located in B2 under any prefix that session tried,
and ghosthacker-shiropico-assets showed a read/write asymmetry (`git ls-remote` succeeded,
`git push` failed with `could not read from remote repository`) that nobody had explained.

Throughout this ADR, VERIFIED PRESENT means an exit code from
`git annex checkpresentkey <key> <remote>` (0 = present, 1 = absent; the command prints
nothing, so parsing stdout yields nothing) or an S3 HEAD returning both ContentLength and
ETag. CLAIMED PRESENT means the git-annex location log, i.e. `whereis`. The two are
different measurements and this ADR never substitutes one for the other.

## 1. kouhou: 10 objects at one copy, and why the second copy did not take

Baseline, by exit code over all 48 keys from `git annex find --format='${key}\\n'`:

    present=38 absent=10 error=0        (checkpresentkey ... kotobase)

while `git annex whereis --key=SHA256E-s1137--5b42cf11...xml` printed `(3 copies)` and
listed kotobase among them. The location log was lying about exactly those 10.

The obvious remedy failed in an instructive way. `git annex copy --to kotobase --force`
(the `--force` is what stops it consulting the location log and deciding the content is
already there) reported `ok` for all 10 and recorded state. Re-probing by exit code gave
present=38 absent=10 again, and the absent set was byte-identical to the first set. So the
location log was not the obstacle; the remote was.

`git annex checkpresentkey --debug` showed why. For a healthy object, one HEAD on the
chunked path returns 200 with `Content-Type: text/xml` and
`ETag: W/\"6fe4d4cc82333dfb3774f8fb776dab83e146e741cb918bcaa20fc88ed6a08e62\"`, and
checkpresent stops there with exit 0. For each of the 10, the same HEAD returns 200 but
with `Content-Type: text/html`, no ETag and no Content-Length; git-annex rejects that,
falls back to the unchunked path, gets 404, and exits 1. A deliberately bogus key returns
404 with `Content-Type: application/xml` on both paths, so the 200/text/html response is
not the generic missing-object reply -- something really is stored there.

The 10 files are HTML error pages that kouhou's feed fetcher saved under `.xml` names
(`gov-afg-exec.xml` is a 403 Forbidden page, `gov-srb-exec.xml` a 301 Moved Permanently
page; 274,760 bytes for all 10). s3.kotobase.net is fronted by Cloudflare, and anything it
serves as text/html goes through Cloudflare's HTML rewriter, which appends the Web
Analytics beacon script. Measured directly with the S3 API:

    LIST   cloud-itonami-kouhou -> object present, Size=1137
    HEAD   same key             -> ContentLength=None, ETag=None
    GET    same key             -> 1496 bytes, sha256 904ca00e...
    local  gov-afg-exec.xml     -> 1137 bytes, sha256 5b42cf11...
    cmp                          -> differ at char 1124, line 7
    tail of the returned bytes  -> ...<script type=\"module\"
                                   src=\"https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js/...\"
                                   data-cf-beacon='{\"version\":\"2024.11.0\",...}'></script></body></html>

So the kotobase copy of those 10 was never a copy. The bytes are rewritten in flight, the
rewriter drops Content-Length and ETag so HEAD cannot confirm anything, and a retrieval
returns content whose checksum does not match the key. `git annex fsck --from kotobase`
on one of them reported `content is missing` and fixed the location log, which is the
correct verdict.

The fix was a second, independent off-machine remote. Using the existing tooling with
credentials resolved by known identifier only:

    ANNEX_ENDPOINT=s3.us-west-004.backblazeb2.com ANNEX_BUCKET=gftdcojp-m365-annex \\
    ANNEX_FILEPREFIX=cloud-itonami-kouhou/ \\
      nbb scripts/datalad-b2-init.cljs orgs/cloud-itonami/kouhou b2
    git annex copy --to b2 --force raw

Then, by exit code over all 48 keys:

    B2 VERIFY present=48 absent=0 error=0 of 48

That is 48 VERIFIED PRESENT, not 48 claimed. The 10 that existed only on this machine now
have a genuine off-machine copy.

The lying log was corrected by the tool that measures rather than the one that asserts:
`git annex fsck --from kotobase --fast raw` re-probed every key and printed
`(fixing location log)` for exactly the 10, ending `fsck: 9 failed` (the tenth had been
fsck'd separately during diagnosis). `git annex setpresentkey` was deliberately not used --
it writes a claim without checking it, which is the failure mode this whole exercise is
about. After the fsck, `whereis` on one of the 10 lists b2 (real, off-machine), the temp
repo at /private/tmp/kouhou-annex (same machine), and here -- kotobase is gone from the
list, correctly.

Finally the map was published: `git push cloud-itonami git-annex:git-annex`, a3c18c3..602f476,
confirmed by `git ls-remote`. The GitHub remote was chosen by inspecting URLs for
github.com, never by `git remote | head -1`, which in this repo would have selected the
`kotobase` special remote.

The 10 corrupted objects still sit in the kotobase bucket. They were not deleted; deleting
annexed content is out of scope.

## 2. product-corpus and cloud-itonami-contact-pii: both found, nothing gone

Neither checkout has a `.git/annex` directory and neither has a `git-annex` branch --
not locally, and `git ls-remote` on their GitHub remotes returns only `main`. That is why a
git-annex-based search finds nothing in them: every git-annex command in a repo with no
annex state reports nothing, truthfully. Their annexed content is present as v7 pointer
blobs in the tree (65-byte blobs reading `/annex/objects/MD5E-...`), so the keys are
recoverable from git even though the location log is not.

The content is in Backblaze B2. A full listing of the bucket `gftdcojp-m365-annex`
(298,106 objects) shows both documented prefixes exist, and every key HEADs successfully:

    product-corpus/                 8 keys, 8 HEAD-200, 0 HEAD-404, sizes exact
                                    (46444, 20243, 40141, 39604, 45719, 52954, 4602, 50875)
    cloud-itonami-contact-pii/      1 key,  1 HEAD-200, ContentLength=334,
                                    LastModified 2026-08-04T06:30:30+00:00
    both prefixes also carry the marker object annex-uuid (36 bytes)

The prefixes queried were `product-corpus/` and `cloud-itonami-contact-pii/` in bucket
`gftdcojp-m365-annex` at endpoint `s3.us-west-004.backblazeb2.com`; both returned their
full expected object set. No other bucket was needed.

cloud-itonami-contact-pii holds exactly one annexed object,
`records/PLUMBING-CHECK.edn.age`, key `MD5E-s334--b3ded98f00e9bf189880e54adf142e38.edn.age`.
It was located by key and byte count only. It was not downloaded, decrypted, printed or
copied, and its contents are not in this ADR.

So this is the briefing's own distinction made concrete: the content was published and the
map never was. Nothing is gone. The remaining gap is that neither repo has a git-annex
branch to push, so it cannot simply be published -- the location log has to be rebuilt by
an `initremote` that adopts the existing `annex-uuid` marker under each prefix, followed by
`git annex fsck --from b2`. That was not done here because a naive `git annex initremote`
against a prefix that already carries an `annex-uuid` marker risks overwriting that marker
in a bucket shared by 30 datasets, and mutating shared custody state uninstructed is not
warranted when the bytes are already safe.

## 3. The read/write asymmetry: the repository is archived

For orgs/gftdcojp/ghosthacker-shiropico-assets, `git ls-remote origin` succeeds and lists
`refs/heads/git-annex` and `refs/heads/main`, while a push fails. The cause is not SSH keys
(both directions use the same `git@github.com:` transport in the same invocation), not a
URL divergence (`remote.origin.url` is `git@github.com:gftdcojp/ghosthacker-shiropico-assets.git`
and no `remote.origin.pushurl` is configured -- `git config --get-regexp '^remote\\.'` shows
only url, fetch, annex-ignore), and not permissions. It is the GitHub archive flag:

    $ git push --dry-run origin git-annex:git-annex
    ERROR: This repository was archived so it is read-only.
    fatal: Could not read from remote repository.

    $ gh api repos/gftdcojp/ghosthacker-shiropico-assets
    {\"archived\":true,\"disabled\":false,\"visibility\":\"private\",
     \"permissions\":{\"admin\":true,\"maintain\":true,\"push\":true,...}}

Two things are worth keeping. First, `permissions.push` is still `true` on an archived
repository, so a permissions check clears a push that GitHub will refuse -- the archive
flag is a separate gate that overrides it. Second, `fatal: Could not read from remote
repository` is git's generic message when the remote helper exits non-zero; it is emitted
for a write refusal and says nothing about reads. Reading the line above it is what
identifies the cause. This matches manifest/repos.edn, which records the repo under
`:archived` (retired 2026-07-29, ADR-2607289700).

Nothing is blocked today: the git-annex branch is in sync with the remote (84bd9ce both
sides) and the content is off-machine -- 40 unique keys across 42 annexed files, all 40
matched in the full B2 inventory under prefix `ghosthacker-shiropico/`, with a 20-key
sample re-verified at present=20 absent=0 by checkpresentkey exit code. When a push is next
needed, the sequence is unarchive, push, re-archive. That was not done here; unarchiving a
deliberately retired repository is the owner's call.

## 4. Sweep for the same class

Candidates were found structurally rather than by name: for every path under `orgs/*/*`,
a `.git/annex` directory, a `.datalad` directory, or a `git-annex` ref (loose or packed).
That yielded 50 repos, a superset of the 25 projects carrying `userdata.datalad: true` in
manifest/west.yml. Annexed keys were read from the tree itself (mode 120000 symlinks and
sub-400-byte blobs whose first line contains `/annex/objects/`), so key counts are correct
even in checkouts where git-annex was never initialised.

Content presence was measured two ways. A complete listing of bucket `gftdcojp-m365-annex`
(298,106 objects) was normalised by stripping the `-S<n>-C<n>` chunk infix and matched
against each repo's key set; and `checkpresentkey` was run per key on smaller sets and as a
random sample on large ones, to confirm the listing and the HEAD agree on B2. Across 12
repos, 226 sampled keys returned present=226 absent=0 error=0, agreeing with the listing
in every case. On B2 -- unlike kotobase -- listing and HEAD do agree.

Repos holding content locally, with off-machine coverage (unique keys):

    kouhou                          48/48   b2       VERIFIED by exit code (this ADR)
    loop-noren                      14/14   kotobase VERIFIED by exit code
    gftd-motion-actor                5/5    b2       inventory match + 5/5 exit code
    media-corpus                   300/300  b2       inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    directory.plc                 4000/4000 b2       inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    eu.europa.eur-lex             1237/1237 b2       inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    gov.govinfo.bulkdata            25/25   b2       inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    jp.go.e-gov.elaws             9524/9524 b2       inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    software.diaspora.nodeinfo   25962/25962 b2      inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    uk.gov.legislation            3493/3493 b2       inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    xyz.farcaster.fnames         14495/14495 b2      inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    ghosthacker-shiropico-assets    40/40   b2       inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    mangaka-data (gftdcojp)         44/44   b2       inventory match + 20/20 exit code
    newsfeed                         1/1    b2       inventory match + 1/1 exit code
    m365-archive                215928 unique keys   200-key random sample, present=200

Checkouts with keys in the tree but no local content (so no single-copy exposure from this
machine), all fully matched in the B2 inventory: ghosthacker-shiropico 328/328,
cloud-itonami-gtm-data 635/635, mangaka-data (cloud-itonami) 44/44, product-corpus 8/8,
cloud-itonami-contact-pii 1/1, tsukuru-manufacturing-artifacts 2/2, training-corpus 2/2,
emr-claims-primary-sources 2/2, jp.go.meti.enecho 2/2, and the kotoba-lang mirror paths of
directory.plc, eu.europa.eur-lex, gov.govinfo.bulkdata, jp.go.e-gov.elaws,
software.diaspora.nodeinfo, uk.gov.legislation and xyz.farcaster.fnames. hayari-data's
68/68 were matched by listing its own kotobase bucket cloud-itonami-hayari-data.

Result: kouhou's 10 were the only content in the workspace at one copy.

Unpublished `git-annex` branches were the more common defect, and four were published as
fast-forward pushes (no force, no history rewriting), each confirmed by `git ls-remote`:

    cloud-itonami/loop-noren            new branch  a87a037   (was local-only, 14 keys)
    etzhayyim/software.diaspora.nodeinfo new branch 4a1d622   (was local-only, 26,470 keys)
    cloud-itonami/mangaka-data          57258e6..23f693a      (pushed via the gftdcojp
                                                               name, which GitHub redirects)
    cloud-itonami/kouhou                a3c18c3..602f476

Still open after the sweep:

  - product-corpus and cloud-itonami-contact-pii have no git-annex branch anywhere, so
    there is nothing to push; their maps must be rebuilt (section 2).
  - kotoba-lang/software.diaspora.nodeinfo, the mirror of the repo whose map was just
    published under etzhayyim, still has no git-annex branch.
  - archive-scans is the one case where content could not be located. Its map IS published
    (52 keys), `remote.log` is empty -- it has no special remote at all -- and every
    location log entry names exactly one repository uuid,
    af748e65-fc58-4e85-8224-fd0754e32bc4, described in uuid.log as
    `Personal ScanSnap document archive`. That uuid matches no `annex.uuid` of any annexed
    repo in this workspace, and the checkout holds no content. So its 52 scans are claimed
    by a single git-annex repository that is not here, and nothing in the workspace can
    confirm they exist.

    QUESTION FOR THE OWNER: which machine and directory holds the git-annex repository with
    uuid af748e65-fc58-4e85-8224-fd0754e32bc4, or, if it is gone, which bucket and prefix
    should archive-scans' 52 objects be pushed to. Both facts are needed; guessing a prefix
    would create a second empty prefix in a shared bucket rather than find the content.

Not probed, and therefore not cleared:

  - The kotobase-side buckets of etzhayyim/directory.plc, software.diaspora.nodeinfo and
    xyz.farcaster.fnames. B2 covers all three completely, so kotobase is a third copy whose
    state was not measured. Given section 1, any object in them whose content sniffs as
    HTML should be assumed corrupt until measured.
  - m365-archive beyond the sample: 215,728 of 215,928 unique keys were not probed. Its
    remote is `encryption=hybrid`, so object names are GPGHMACSHA1 hashes and cannot be
    matched against the bucket listing; only per-key checkpresentkey works, at roughly one
    HTTPS round trip each. The 200-key sample was clean but it is a sample.
  - hayari-data was matched by bucket listing only; no per-key HEAD was run, and after
    section 1 a kotobase listing is weaker evidence than a kotobase HEAD.
  - Repos with zero annexed keys, where there was nothing to probe: gftd-audio-actor,
    gftd-avatar-actor, gftd-illust-actor, gftd-rig-actor, gftd-sculpt-actor,
    gftd-voice-actor, hirameki-patents, gftd-llm-dataset, gov.sec.edgar,
    org.ourworldindata, org.un.unstats, org.worldbank.api.
  - Every west project that is not checked out on this machine. The sweep covered what is
    on disk under `orgs/*/*` plus the 25 west datalad entries; the remaining ~4,100
    projects were not examined and an annexed repo among them would not have been seen.
  - kouhou's temp-directory copy, uuid e0062bdc-b271-46dc-b42a-5d3c0768266e at
    /private/tmp/kouhou-annex, is still claimed in the location log. It is on this machine
    and in /private/tmp, so it is not custody. It was left alone; removing a claim is a
    drop-shaped operation and out of scope.

## Decision

1. Custody claims are settled by exit code or by an S3 HEAD that returns both
   ContentLength and ETag. `whereis` output is a claim and is never quoted as a count.
2. When a location log is wrong, correct it with `git annex fsck --from <remote>`, which
   measures. Do not use `git annex setpresentkey`, which asserts.
3. `git annex copy --to <remote> --force` is the transfer that ignores the location log.
   If it reports ok and a re-probe still says absent, the remote is at fault, not the log.
4. Do not store annexed content on s3.kotobase.net when that content may sniff as HTML.
   Cloudflare's HTML rewriter injects the Web Analytics beacon, which strips ETag and
   Content-Length from HEAD and changes the bytes returned by GET, so the object fails both
   verification and retrieval. Until the facade sends a non-sniffable Content-Type or
   disables the rewriter for this host, a B2 copy is required alongside any kotobase copy.
5. Content and the map are two publications. A repo is only in custody when the objects are
   verified off-machine AND `git ls-remote <github-remote> refs/heads/git-annex` returns a
   ref. Select that remote by matching github.com in the URL.
6. A push failing with `fatal: Could not read from remote repository` is not evidence about
   reads. Read the line above it, and check `gh api repos/<org>/<repo> --jq .archived`
   before blaming keys or permissions.")))
